27 Can our phones keep us safe? A content analysis of smartphone 28 applications to prevent mobile phone distracted driving

29

Mobile phone use while driving is a pervasive problem that continues to increase,

30

notwithstanding the large crash risk this behaviour constitutes. A number of phone

31

applications have been developed with the intention of utilising the technology to prevent

32

dangerous phone behaviours while driving. Despite the potential these applications have

33

in preventing crashes associated with distracted driving, research is yet to explore these

34

emergent applications. Therefore, this study provided a review of the current smartphone

35

applications developed to prevent distracted driving. A content analysis was conducted

36

to identify the smartphone applications targeted at stopping, preventing or reducing

37

phone use behaviour while driving. Their functionality was determined based on the

38

ecosystem of smartphone applications: application-mobile phone interaction,

39

application-driver interaction, and application-context interaction. A total of 29 relevant

40

applications in English language were identified. Most of these applications focused on

41

blocking specific phone functions (e.g. texting or calling) while allowing more desirable

42

driving phone functions to be accessed (e.g. music applications and GPS functions). The

43

specific functions which are blocked or allowed varied greatly between applications. Out

44

of the different application interactions, the function which sends an automatic text

45

message to a contact who texts the driver (associated with external communicator

46

interactions) was the most common feature. A major limitation of the applications was

47

their reliance on blocking specific phone functions as opposed to managing workload

48

while driving or simplifying specific phone tasks to be more compatible with driving.

49

Simply blocking phone functions may not be attractive to drivers who view their phone

50

as a necessity. As such, these drivers are unlikely to use these voluntary applications at

51

all while driving. Smartphone applications designed to prevent phone use while driving

52

show potential for playing a large role in a systemic intervention to prevent mobile phone

53

distracted driving, yet there is a substantial need for further development of these

54

applications.

