64 1. Introduction

65 Mobile phone distracted driving is a type of inattention that occurs when drivers divert (or

66 share) their attention away from the driving task to focus on a mobile phone activity. Mobile

67 phone use while driving is a global concern that is estimated to increase crash risk. Based on

68 crash reports in the U.S., it is believed that distracted driving (including mobile phone use) was

69 involved in nearly 10% of all traffic fatalities in 2014 (NHTSA, 2016). In addition, a study of

70 police crash reports found that mobile phone distraction resulted in 18% of fatal crashes and

71 5% of injury crashes (Overton et al., 2014). Asbridge et al. (2013) reported that the odds of a

72 culpable crash increase by 70% when the driver is using a mobile phone.

73

On-road studies are also utilised to investigate changes in crash risk as a consequence

74 of mobile phone use while driving. The most recent and largest naturalistic study in the U.S.,

75 Second Strategic Highway Research Program Naturalistic Driving Study (SHRP 2 NDS),

76 gathered information from more than 3,500 drivers across a 3-year period (Dingus et al., 2016).

77 The SHRP 2 NDS has reported that browsing, handheld dialling, and handheld text interactions

78 with a mobile phone significantly increase the odds of crash risk by a factor of 2.7-12.2 (Dingus

79 et al., 2016). On the other hand, cognitive-manual (e.g. handheld mobile phone conversations)

80 and cognitive (e.g. hands-free mobile phone conversations) mobile phone interactions were not

81 found to be associated with increased crash risk after controlling for “additional task” selection

82 bias and “driver behaviour error” confounding bias (Young, 2017). These results have been

83 further documented in both a recent meta-analysis (Simmons et al. 2016) and recent systematic

84 review (Oviedo-Trespalacios et al. 2016) of on-road and naturalistic studies. The threat that

85 mobile phone use while driving poses, particularly visual-manual mobile phone use, makes

86 this one of the most important issues in road safety worldwide.

87

Little success has been reported worldwide in stopping mobile phone use while driving.

88 Highly-developed road safety systems in countries such as Australia, the U.K, Spain, and

89 Germany, with less than 6 road fatalities per 100,000 inhabitants per year (World Health 90 Organization, 2015), have been unable to fully prevent distracted driving. For instance, a cross91 sectional study in Australia showed that one in every two drivers (n = 443) engaged in an illegal 92 task such as handheld conversations or texting/browsing while driving in a typical day 93 (Oviedo-Trespalacios et al., 2017). In Spain, a study using roadside interviews (n = 426) found 94 that 32.2% of drivers reported having handheld conversations and 43.7% reported sending text 95 messages while driving (Prat et al., 2017). These studies report mobile phone use sometime 96 during a trip or during a day, whereas observational studies indicate how many drivers are 97 using mobile phones at a given time and location. In the U.K, an observational study of 10,984 98 drivers at random locations observed that nearly 2.7% of drivers were talking on a handheld or 99 hands-free mobile phone (Sullman et al., 2014). Likewise, in Germany, an observational study

100 (including 11,837 observations) detected a prevalence of 4.5% for visual-manual interactions 101 such as texting or browsing while driving (Vollrath et al., 2016). It is important to note that 102 many of these countries have legislation that prohibit handheld mobile phone use while driving, 103 however, legislation has had a minimal effect due to the difficulties in enforcing the rule (Nevin 104 et al., 2016) and enforcement-avoidance strategies used by drivers (Oviedo-Trespalacios, 105 2018). Distracted driving affects population health by increasing crash risk, and research 106 exploring potential countermeasures is still much needed.

107

An emergent opportunity for distracted driving prevention is presented by smartphone

108 applications designed to prevent mobile phone use while driving. The common rationale across

109 these smartphone applications is that the mobile phones could prevent interactions while the

110 vehicle is moving (Siuhi and Mwakalonge, 2016). A common example is the “Do Not Disturb

111 While Driving” application developed by Apple (now preinstalled in iPhones worldwide) with

112 the promise of helping drivers keep their eyes on the road by disabling phone functions such

113 as texting once the iPhone senses vehicle motion (APPLE Inc., 2017). Reducing off-road

114 glances while in control of a vehicle is very positive in terms of safety, given that sharing visual

115 attention between mobile phone tasks and driving has been associated with crashes and

116 impaired vehicle control (NHTSA, 2016, Simmons et al., 2016, Oviedo-Trespalacios et al.,

117 2016, Oviedo-Trespalacios et al., 2018a). However, there is little evidence regarding the

118 effectiveness of voluntarily initiated smartphone applications in preventing mobile phone

119 related road crashes. A plethora of issues remain to be assessed concerning the capability of

120 these smartphone applications to fully stop risky mobile phone use while driving and the

121 intentions/acceptability of drivers regarding their use. Firstly, mobile phone use while driving

122 is a complex activity (Oviedo-Trespalacios et al., 2017, Oviedo-Trespalacios et al., 2016), as

123 drivers can engage in a wide range of activities that can increase crash risk (e.g., texting or

124 browsing) as well as support driving (e.g. GPS or weather alerts). Secondly, research has

125 suggested that some drivers develop maladaptive attachment to their mobile phones (Hong et

126 al., 2012, Weller et al., 2013), which would reduce their willingness to activate these

127 smartphone applications. Therefore, it is necessary to gain a better understanding of the

128 potential safety benefits of these smartphone applications as well as the features that could

129 influence their use among drivers.

130

This paper aims to review current smartphone applications designed to prevent

131 distracted driving. A methodology was designed to investigate two main topics: (i) the safety

132 implications of the features currently available in smartphone applications; (ii) the potential

133 effectiveness of the smartphone applications to reduce mobile phone distraction. Finally,

134 research gaps and future research were identified to support the implementation of smartphone

135 applications as a tool to stop mobile phone distracted driving.

136 2. Methods
137 A content analysis of web pages promoting smartphone applications designed to prevent 138 mobile phone use while driving was conducted. Content analysis is a methodology for making 139 inferences from qualitative data by systematically characterizing its themes and has been 140 frequently used in the analysis of websites, e.g. retirement village websites (Hu et al., 2017), 141 cosmetic and anti-aging products and treatments websites (Rachul et al., 2015), and fast-food 142 websites (Wellard et al., 2017). 143 144 2.1. Search Strategy 145 To identify the smartphone applications to be reviewed, a search on the Google search engine 146 was conducted using the keywords “stop” AND “distracted” AND “driving” AND “app”. All 147 the web pages reviewed were in English. Each web page was reviewed to identify potential 148 smartphone applications including pages describing a specific application or listing a group of

149 applications. Every smartphone application mentioned in the aforementioned web pages was

150 searched consecutively. Additional searches were conducted in the Google Play store for

151 Android (85% of smartphone market) and the Apple iTunes store for iOS (14.7% of

152 smartphone market) using the keywords listed above and also reviewing other suggested

153 smartphone applications to identify the available applications in the market (see

154 https://www.idc.com/promo/smartphone-market-share/os). As exclusion criteria, only

155 smartphone applications targeted at stopping, preventing, or reducing mobile phone use while

156 driving were considered and only web pages in English were used. The searches were

157 conducted by the first author and the eligibility of the unique applications was decided through

158 discussion among all the co-authors. A total of 36 unique applications were identified, of which

159 32 were eligible. Finally, three applications were discontinued during the study and therefore

160 removed (see appendix).

161

162 2.1. Information processing

163 To investigate the functionality of smartphone applications, a coding sheet was designed based

164 on the ecosystem of smartphone applications to prevent mobile phone use while driving (see

165 Figure 1). The coding sheet was developed in an iterative process. First, one author randomly

166 selected 15 applications and initially coded the included definitions to develop a preliminary

167 coding sheet. The preliminary coding sheet was based on the view of mobile phone distracted

168 driving as a human-machine system proposed by Oviedo-Trespalacios et al. (2016), which

169 suggests two levels of interactions: (i) smartphone application to prevent mobile phone use

170 while driving and the human-machine system components (i.e., driver, car, and mobile phone)

171 and (ii) smartphone application to prevent mobile phone use while driving and the

172 environment. The coding system was revised through discussion among the authors. Next, all

173 applications were coded following a two-step analysis where two researchers independently

174 coded each application and continuously extending the coding sheet if new codes emerged

175 during the analysis of new applications. A third reviewer was consulted to resolve any

176 disagreement in extracted information such as inconsistency in characteristics of smartphone

177 applications or interaction level (e.g., missing information). Following the recommendations

178 of Hsieh and Shannon (2005), the codes were discussed within the research team and grouped

179 into meaningful clusters. The searches and data extraction were conducted in December 2017.

180 In order to provide updated information regarding the applications in terms of number version

181 and download, two researchers updated this information on an ongoing basis. The last update

182 was conducted in April 2018.

