214 3. Results
215 A total of 29 applications advertised to stop/prevent/reduce distracted driving were identified. 216 The applications and their main characteristics are presented in Table 2. The most common 217 Android application was Android Auto. Given the “Do Not Disturb While Driving” function 218 is pre-installed in all iOS 11 devices, this was the most common application for iOS phones. 219 AT&T DriveMode was the most common application that was available for both Android and 220 iOS devices. Almost all applications (n = 28) were available in Android phone systems. 221 Meanwhile, 11 applications were available for iOS phone systems and only 2 applications were 222 available on Blackberry phone systems. A total of 15 applications were free to use, 4 223 applications needed to be purchased in order to be used and 10 applications included a mix of 224 free use and the requirement to provide payment for use; this mostly involved either a free 225 demonstration or the application was free to download but included in-application purchases.
