258 4. Discussion

259

There are a great number of smartphone applications that are promoted for the

260 prevention of mobile phone distracted driving. The content analysis confirmed that most

261 of the smartphone applications available today are focused on blocking functions such as 262 texting, browsing or handheld conversations. This focus on blocking visual-manual tasks 263 is encouraging, considering that recent studies have consistently identified the risks 264 associated with drivers taking their eyes off the road to use a mobile phone (NHTSA, 265 2016, Simmons et al., 2016, Oviedo-Trespalacios et al., 2016). Research has suggested 266 that some drivers using smartphone applications such as DriveSafe.ly might reduce their 267 exposure to texting (McGinn, 2014). However, a number of applications to prevent 268 mobile phone use while driving still allow texting applications, some attached to 269 applications that are not primarily text based, e.g., Facebook Messenger, Viber,

270 WhatsApp, WeChat, etc.

271

Another issue is that mobile phone devices have significantly changed over the

272 years to include technologies that allow engagement in a wide range of activities such as

273 navigating social media (e.g., Instagram, tinder, Facebook, twitter), GPS, listening to

274 music, and internet gaming, all on one device, which has increased mobile phone use in

275 our lives (Ayar et al., 2017). Findings from this research show that applications to prevent

276 mobile phone use while driving might not fully prevent visual-manual interactions such

277 as in-car streaming music interfaces or GPS devices, which is not always compatible with

278 driving. Recent studies found that young drivers have a strong intention to use music and

279 GPS phone functions while driving (George et al., 2018, Truelove et al., 2019). There is

280 a need to define strategies that support the use of these functions in a risk-minimising way

281 while driving.

282

Functions targeting involuntary distraction remain relatively underdeveloped in

283 smartphone applications to prevent distracted driving. Involuntary distraction involves

284 sudden non-driving information or stimuli originated from the mobile phone that captures

285 the driver’s attention, i.e., notifications of incoming text messages, email or calls.

286 Incoming calls and, less frequently, notifications are permitted in most of the smartphone

287 applications identified in this review. A sudden incoming call/notification represents a

288 greater risk for traffic safety compared to a self-initiated interaction. Externally triggered

289 tasks do not consider current driving demands and prevent drivers from managing

290 workload (Oviedo-Trespalacios et al., 2017, Oviedo-Trespalacios et al., 2018a, Oviedo-

291 Trespalacios et al., 2019). Results from recent naturalistic studies confirm that activities

292 such as reaching for a mobile phone can increase crash risk (odds ratio of 4.8) (Dingus et

293 al., 2016). Findings from this study show that not many applications manage incoming

294 calls. Some smartphone applications restrict incoming calls to the use of hands-free

295 interfaces (e.g., cellMUTE+ Drive Mode), which avoid visual-manual demands. On the

296 other hand, some applications restrict incoming calls to a limited selection of contacts

297 (e.g., DriveAlertNow), which could minimises exposure. There are no applications that

298 help drivers to safely manage an incoming call based on the level of driving demand being

299 experienced.

300

One of the most important findings of this investigation is that the smartphone

301 applications developed for preventing mobile distracted driving lack variety. The

302 widespread task-blocking approach is not flexible enough to safely manage instrumental

303 mobile phone interactions with Maps or Music applications. In addition, blocking tasks

304 might not benefit some groups of the population that have developed strong habits of

305 mobile phone use and, therefore, might not accept this technology (Bianchi and Phillips,

306 2005). Studies conducted in Australia and the U.S. have demonstrated that “mobile phone

307 blockers” have low acceptability among drivers, which reduces technology adoption

308 (Delgado et al., 2018, Ponte et al., 2016). An option to overcome this limitation is to shift

309 the mobile phone task-blocking paradigm towards the design of strategies seeking better

310 compatibility between mobile phone tasks and driving. Authorities such as the NHTSA

311 (2016) have reported that the development of technologies dedicated to reducing driver

312 workload (i.e., visual, manual, and cognitive demands) associated with performing

313 secondary tasks is a worthwhile approach to increase safety.

314

Smartphone applications that facilitate safer mobile phone use while driving are

315 in the early stages of design and development. However, findings from the content

316 analysis confirm that some smartphone applications have started to incorporate safe

317 mobile phone-driving integration principles into their designs. Two main approaches

318 have been considered: (i) changing the nature of the interaction between the driver and

319 the phone; and (ii) simplifying smartphone functionality. The nature of the interaction

320 can be changed by the use of voice commands instead of the visual-manual interface, and

321 is emerging in smartphone applications such as Do Not Disturb While Driving and

322 DriveSafe.ly, which are capable of reading text aloud and responding to texts by voice.

323 As explained earlier, visual-manual interactions are a major risk for mobile phone

324 distracted drivers (Dingus et al., 2016). Although some studies have concluded that the

325 impact of phone conversations is negligible (Young, 2017, Simmons et al., 2016, Oviedo-

326 Trespalacios et al., 2016), there are concerns that the cognitive demands of conversations

327 might be risky under certain circumstances such as difficult conversations (Lansdown

328 and Stephens, 2013). Nonetheless, exchanging visual demands for audio/cognitive

329 demands is a viable risk-minimising strategy. Unfortunately, the technology is not always

330 reliable and drivers are not likely to use voice commands to send text message unless they

331 are experiencing adverse driving conditions (Tchankue et al., 2012). Low reliability and

332 accuracy of the technology is likely to undermine widespread implementation of these

333 smartphone applications. However, mobile phones are evolving to have increased

334 capabilities to monitor driving behaviour (Kanarachos et al., 2018), which will certainly

335 benefit future developments in this area.

336

On the other hand, simplification of mobile phone functions is a new concept

337 which enables drivers to engage in a wide range of tasks including texting. The

338 smartphone application Safe Driving App: Drivemode is the only application identified

339 in this study based on this concept. Drivemode allows a wide range of activities from

340 driving related applications such as GPS to social media interactions and texting. In

341 addition, Drivemode uses simplified interfaces to integrate the mobile tasks into driving

342 with the aim of reducing visual and motor demands during driver-phone interactions, e.g.,

343 providing shortcuts to relevant actions that the driver performs with his/her mobile phone.

344 In addition, Drivemode has a number of voice-based commands to substitute driver

345 demands. Although applications supporting safe integration between mobile phone tasks

346 and driving are becoming more widespread, caution is still required in recommending

347 their implementation. At present, there is no empirical evidence that these workload

348 manager applications are able to reduce crash risk. In addition, concerns remains that,

349 under some road traffic conditions, drivers may not have enough spare capacity to

350 perform even simplified mobile phone tasks. Smartphone applications to support mobile

351 phone integration while driving do not have awareness of driving demands and, therefore,

352 cannot facilitate or inhibit driver decisions to engage in mobile phone tasks or not. This

353 is concerning, as research has demonstrated that driver judgement is not always adequate

354 among some high-risk groups such as novice drivers (Oviedo-Trespalacios et al., 2018b).

355

Interactions between the external communicators and the smartphone applications

356 are an important influence in the applications’ functioning. Smartphone applications

357 frequently interact with external communicators in two ways: to inform them that the

358 driver is unavailable to engage in texting or talking or to redirect the communication

359 activity (e.g., transfer to voice mail). Typically, external communicators will receive a

360 message, often personalised, informing them that the person that they are contacting is

361 driving, and they have the chance to decide whether to still engage in a texting activity

362 (i.e., resend the message) or change the interface (e.g., substitute the text message with a

363 mobile phone call). External communicators with a good understanding of the risks would

364 serve as key actors in moderating driver exposure to mobile phone interactions.

365 Therefore, it is important to broaden educational efforts to educate external

366 communicators of their role in preventing crashes due to mobile phone use while driving.

367

In this research it was identified that smartphone applications have external users

368 who are not directly trying to communicate with the drivers. These external users might

369 include family, friends or employers of the driver. An example of this is the

370 DriverAlertNow application that is promoted as having the capability to inform parents

371 of the mobile phone use behaviours of young drivers or whether the application has been

372 overridden/deactivated. Similar performance sharing arrangements are promoted to fleet

373 companies with the intention of providing surveillance of the driving performance of

374 employees (see Fleetmode application). Although such applications seem to operate

375 mostly on a punishment model, some promising approaches based on rewards are also

376 emerging among available applications. A good example of this is the Text ninja

377 application that is being used for advocacy groups to create collaborative awareness

378 campaigns where a group of drivers can monitor their performance and measure the

379 benefits from using applications to prevent distracting driving. Approaches using

380 incentives have been reported to be effective in improving driver behaviour, for example

381 the Speed Camera Lottery in Stockholm, Sweden (see Schultz (2010)), and various types

382 of Pay As You Drive vehicle insurance schemes (Tselentis et al., 2016). Moreover, incentives

383 may also increase acceptance of technology and could help to support implementation of

384 in-car systems (Vaezipour et al., 2017). This has implications for insurance organizations

385 that could potentially use the information to offer rewards and ultimately save lives.

386 Nonetheless, concerns about privacy could also play an important role in discouraging

387 users (Khalid et al., 2015). Research is necessary to determine schemes of private data

388 management that guarantee driver acceptance while ensuring efficiency in reducing

389 exposure to mobile phone distraction.

390

There is a need for communication between the mobile phone and the vehicle.

391 New vehicles today have the capability to support mobile phone use through vehicle

392 audio systems (also known as integrated hands-free). This is very convenient given that

393 installing external hands-free devices is often a barrier due to cost or lack of practicality.

394 The vehicle could also communicate with the smartphone applications to guarantee a

395 timely activation of the applications just before the vehicle starts moving. Automatic

396 activation of the smartphone application to prevent distracted driving is more reliable than

397 the driver having to launch the application before every drive.

398

The smartphone applications to prevent mobile use while driving identified in this

399 study are voluntary and for personal use. To date, there is little research on drivers’

400 intention to use these smartphone applications to prevent mobile phone use while driving.

401 Previous work has suggested that many of these smartphone applications are difficult to

402 use and not always reliable (Sousa, 2015, Tchankue et al., 2012). In addition, previous

403 efforts using psychosocial variables (i.e., attitudes, likelihood of engaging and frequency

404 of reported texting and driving behaviours) to predict the use of smartphone applications

405 to reduce mobile phone distracted driving have had little success (McGinn, 2014).

406 Another important finding was that nearly half of the applications are free, which might

407 influence drivers’ adoption of this technology. Technology acceptance models for in-

408 vehicle technology have highlighted the importance of affordability in the acceptance of

409 new technologies (Mitsopoulos-Rubens and Regan, 2017). Acceptance of the technology

410 is critical given that many of these applications can be easily overridden or disabled by

411 the driver at any time (e.g. ”Do Not Disturb While Driving”), which makes the technology

412 highly dependent on the willingness of the driver to use the application. This highlights

413 the need for a more comprehensive understanding of drivers’ requirements and involving

414 them in the design of these smartphone applications to facilitate greater acceptance and

415 adoption of the technology. The importance of user involvement in designing new

416 technologies has been highlighted previously (DIS, 2009, François et al., 2017).

417

Lastly, it is very encouraging that more stakeholders are systematically

418 collaborating in the design and development of technology to prevent distracted driving.

419 The role of mobile phone manufacturers and application designers in preventing mobile

420 phone distracted driving has tended to be overlooked. Mobile phone companies are seen

421 often as complacent about their responsibility (Galitz, 2018). To date, interventions to

422 stop mobile phone distraction while driving have been heavily focused on the role of the

423 driver, while ignoring the responsibility of the wider road transport or communication

424 authorities (Young and Salmon, 2015, Parnell et al., 2017, Parnell et al., 2016). A

425 systemic approach is more likely to succeed in preventing mobile phone use while

426 driving. This research confirms that the smartphone applications to prevent mobile phone

427 distracted driving can be an important part of a system wide approach involving industry,

428 government, community, and family, given their capabilities to record information and

429 block risky interactions such as visual-manual tasks.

430 5. Conclusion

431

This paper reviewed current smartphone applications designed to prevent

432 distracted driving. The results show that the available smartphone applications use

433 blocking approaches rather than manage the workload of mobile phone interactions while

434 driving. This limits the success of the applications given that mobile phone use is

435 becoming more pervasive. Nonetheless, these smartphone applications show the potential

436 of reducing exposure to risky behaviours such as taking one’s eyes off the road.

437

Current approaches to application design show the potential for these applications

438 to interact with aspects of the driver ecosystem such as the vehicle and external

439 communicators and users. This confirms that smartphone applications have the capability

440 to be part of large systemic intervention to prevent mobile phone distraction. However,

441 there are a great number of applications, including some that do not necessarily prevent

442 risky interactions, as demonstrated in this investigation. In order to guarantee a reduction

443 in mobile phone use while driving, a definition of standard practice for the design of these

444 applications is necessary.

445

One limitation of this study was that user reviews and satisfaction were not

446 considered. In addition, there is no guarantee that the functions mentioned by the

447 applications work appropriately every time. Future research is necessary to measure user

448 perspectives and the usability of the applications as well as the reliability of the

449 smartphone applications.

